      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate for water meters (R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01).\n\nThis is part 2 of 4, so it's a fragment. The fragment contains:\n\n1. **Software versions tables** (pages 5):\n   - Software part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality) with versions 03.00.03, 03.00.04, 03.02.00, 03.03.02, 03.03.03\n   - Software part code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater) with versions 02.00.02, 02.00.04\n   - Software part code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater) with versions 02.00.01, 02.00.05\n\n2. **Table 4** - Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant[1]:\n   - Measurement MCU: 01.02.00, 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.03\n   - 4G Communication MCU: 01.02.00, 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.02, 01.05.01\n   - NB-IoT Communication MCU: 01.03.00, 01.03.01, 01.03.02, 01.03.03\n   - Footnote [1]: The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes.\n\n3. **Table 5** - General characteristics, Reduced bore - AC mains powered: DN40-DN600 with accuracy classes 1 and 2, flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n\n4. **Table 6** - General characteristics, Reduced bore - Battery/Renewable energy powered: same structure.\n\nCertificate metadata from headers:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 13\n- Project number: 3939609\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Page 5 of 13, Page 6 of 13, Page 7 of 13 → page_total: 13\n\nIssuing authority: NMi (header shows \"NMi\")\n\nRecommendation: R49 (2013 edition - \"R49/2013\" and references \"OIML R49-1:2013\")\n\nNow, how to structure the model family. The cert covers a family of instruments (AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z is one variant mentioned). The tables give characteristics per meter size (DN40–DN600) per accuracy class (1 or 2), and per power supply configuration (AC mains vs Battery/Renewable energy).\n\nPer the schema:\n- `model_family.models[]` — one entry per variant. Here the axes are: meter size (DN40...DN600), accuracy class (1, 2), power supply (AC mains, Battery/Renewable). I could create model IDs like \"DN40-class1-AC\" but that might be over-engineering. Let me think about what's most useful.\n\nThe R137 example shows: models as the size, and `model_level` attributes for varying values. Here, there are two config axes: power supply type (AC mains vs battery) and accuracy class. This fits better with `config_level` perhaps, or `matrix_tables` for the raw tables plus model_family with size-based models.\n\nApproach:\n- `model_family.family_name`: \"AquaMaster4\" — wait, is that stated? Table 4 says \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". The certified type designation includes FEW45Y.Z probably. The family name \"AquaMaster4\" is mentioned. Hmm, but this is a fragment; part 1 might have the full details. I should only extract what's present in THIS fragment. The fragment mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" — I can include model_designations from what's seen: FEW45Y.Z. But careful — the cert covers the whole family; Table 5/6 cover \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\".\n\nLet me structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"revision\": 13,  // schema doesn't have revision in certificate... schema has number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision is not listed. Hmm. The revision_history is for document revisions. The header says \"revision 13\". Maybe include in revision_history: [{revision: \"13\", ...}]? Not exactly — revision history is for changes. I could put revision in certificate.number? Like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 13\"? Better keep number clean and note revision... The schema for certificate doesn't include revision field. But rule 8 says omit what's not present; including extra fields might be acceptable but the schema is strict. Let me look: \"certificate: number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id\". Scheme is \"A\" (R49/2013-A-...). project_number: 3939609. page_total: 13. member_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n    \n    For revision, I could add \"revision\": \"13\" — the schema says don't fabricate but doesn't forbid extra fields. I think adding revision to certificate is helpful and true. Actually the revision_history field expects entries; the header \"revision 13\" is the current revision. I'll add \"revision\": \"13\" to certificate as an extra field. Hmm, risk of schema validation failing? The instructions say \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema\". Extra fields are risky. But losing revision 13 info is also bad. I'll include it as \"revision\": \"13\" — many implementations allow additional properties. Alternatively include in revision_history as {\"revision\": \"13\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}? That seems worse. I'll go with certificate.revision.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIssuing authority: NMi — name \"NMi\" (Netherlands Meetinstituut). The fragment only shows \"NMi\" as a header. person_responsible etc. not present → omit. address_lines not present → omit. I'll include name and oiml_issuer_id? Not present. Just name.\n\ncertified_type: \n- category: \"Water meter\" — hmm, the R49 recommendation is water meters. But this fragment doesn't literally say \"water meter\"... Table 5/6 say \"family of instruments\", \"Meter size\". The category per examples: \"Gas meter\" for R137. R49 = Water meters. I think \"Water meter\" is safe and correct. But rule: \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The meters are water meters (R49, m³/h flows, meter size DN). The fragment strongly implies it. Also \"AquaMaster4\" - aqua = water. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: \"FEW45Y.Z\" (from Table 4 title: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\"). Also \"AquaMaster4\" as family name. I'll put type_designations: [\"FEW45Y.Z\"].\n- module_designation: not applicable here → maybe null/omit.\n- description: The cert covers family of instruments; reduced bore; AC mains powered and battery/renewable energy powered variants. I can write a description from Table 5/6 titles.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\" (mentioned in Table 4).\n- models: The variants. Axes: meter size × accuracy class × power supply. Creating one model per combination would be ~26×2... Table 5 has 24 rows (size+class combos), Table 6 has 26 rows. Total 50 combos. That's a lot but doable programmatically. However, for a fragment response, maybe better to represent models as meter sizes (DN40..DN600) and use config_level for accuracy class and power supply axes. Actually accuracy class is a per-model attribute; power supply is a config axis.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what best fits the schema:\n\n- models: one per meter size? But then model_level values per accuracy class... The accuracy class itself varies. The R137 example had one model per size with attributes varying per size. Here flow rates vary per (size, class, power).\n\nOption A: models = size×class×power combos (50 models). model_level attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class, power_supply. That's massive JSON but complete.\n\nOption B: matrix_tables for Table 5 and Table 6 verbatim (rows per size+class), plus model_family.models as sizes, and model_level for q1/q2/q3/q4 with model_id encoding size+class+power... \n\nOption C: models as size+class combos per power config via config_level axis \"power_supply\". So models: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", ... with attributes q1,q2,q3,q4,ratio. And config_level for power supply differences? But power supply changes the flow rate tables entirely, not just a few attributes.\n\nGiven the tables are cleanly preserved in matrix_tables, and model_level entries are per attribute × model, I think the cleanest is:\n\n- model_family.models: entries like {\"model_id\": \"DN40-class1-AC-mains\"}, ... Hmm, that's heavy.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The schema's intent: matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The flow rate tables are exactly that: 4 dimensions (size, class, power, and 5 columns). I'll emit two matrix_tables entries (Table 5 and Table 6) with full rows.\n\nFor model_family and model_level: I'll use model_id = \"DN40-class1\" style? But then power axis conflicts: DN40 class 1 has different flows in AC vs battery. So model_id must include power. E.g., \"DN40|class1|AC\", \"DN40|class1|battery\". That's 50 models. Emitting 50 models × 5 attributes = 250 value entries in model_level. That's a lot of JSON but it's the faithful extraction. Hmm, the instruction says matrix_tables are for tables that \"don't fit elsewhere\" — these do fit model_level if I define models as full combos. But 250 entries is verbose. Alternatively, keep model_level minimal and rely on matrix_tables.\n\nGiven the guidance, I think a balanced approach:\n- matrix_tables: Table 5 and Table 6 fully preserved (with footnotes describing the free-choice conditions).\n- model_family.models: one entry per size×class×power combo? Or per size?\n\nLet me look at the R137 example again: models per size, model_level for the varying attributes. The power supply here acts like a second family dimension. I could treat each table as a \"variant group\" and set models to combos. I'll go with combos encoded like \"DN40-class1-AC\" and \"DN40-class1-BAT\".\n\nActually wait — for readability and to mirror certificate content, model_id strings like \"DN40 class 1 AC mains\" are clearer. Let me use a compact convention: \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN40-AC-C2\", \"DN40-BAT-C1\"... Hmm.\n\nI'll use: \"DN40 class 1 (AC mains)\", \"DN40 class 2 (AC mains)\", ..., \"DN40 class 1 (battery/renewable)\", etc. That's self-describing.\n\nThat's 24 + 26 = 50 models. Let me enumerate Table 5 rows (AC mains):\n1. DN40, class 1: Q1=0.05, Q2=0.08, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 500\n2. DN40, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 1000\n3. DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n4. DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n5. DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n6. DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n7. DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n8. DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n9. DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n10. DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n11. DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n12. DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n13. DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n14. DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n15. DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n16. DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n17. DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n18. DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n19. DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n20. DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n21. DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n22. DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n23. DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n24. DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nThat's 24 rows in Table 5 (I count: DN40×2, DN50×2, DN65×1, DN80×1, DN100×2, DN125×2, DN150×1, DN200×1, DN250×2, DN300×2, DN350×2, DN400×1, DN450×1, DN500×2, DN600×2 = 24). Good.\n\nTable 6 (Battery/Renewable):\n1. DN40, 1&2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n2. DN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n3. DN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n4. DN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n5. DN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n6. DN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n7. DN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n8. DN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n9. DN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n10. DN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n11. DN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n12. DN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n13. DN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n14. DN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n15. DN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n16. DN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n17. DN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n18. DN350, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n19. DN400, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n20. DN400, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n21. DN450, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n22. DN450, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n23. DN500, 1: 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n24. DN500, 2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n25. DN600, 1: 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n26. DN600, 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\n26 rows.\n\nTotal 50 combos. Emitting all as models + model_level would be huge. Given this is one fragment of 4 and the response size matters, I'll rely primarily on matrix_tables for these two tables and provide model_family.models per combo... Hmm.\n\nDecision: I'll include:\n- matrix_tables with the two tables fully (50 rows total).\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models as list — I'll include per-size-class-power combos but maybe abbreviated? If I include 50 model entries as {\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1 AC mains\"} that's fine, it's just names.\n- characteristics.model_level: 5 attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1) × 50 models = 250 entries. Too much. \n\nAlternative: skip model_level for these, since matrix_tables fully capture them? But the schema emphasizes model_level for varying attributes. The R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level. But the R137 example only had 2 models × 3 attributes = 6 entries. Here 50 models.\n\nCompromise: put model_level with the key attributes but reference matrix rows via model_id strings, i.e. emit all 250? That's enormous. I think given the fragment instructions (\"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present\"), and the practical guidance that matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables, I'll do:\n\n- matrix_tables: both tables complete with rows including size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio.\n- model_family.models: 50 combos? Or 12 sizes?\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe treat \"models\" as the size variants and use config_level for the two axes: accuracy class and power supply. config_level entry:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_size × accuracy_class × power_supply\",\n  \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"DN40, class 1, AC mains\", \"value\": 0.05, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}, ...]\n}\n```\n\nThat's the same 250 values again.\n\nOK let me just be pragmatic: the cleanest machine-usable representation preserving all data without explosion:\n\n1. matrix_tables (2 tables, all rows). ✔ complete data.\n2. model_family with models per combo (50 short entries). ✔ enables referencing.\n3. characteristics.model_level — emit only for... hmm, if models are combos, then q1..q4 and ratio vary per model. All 5 attributes vary across all 50. To avoid 250 entries I could emit model_level anyway — it is what the schema wants. 250 entries × ~5 tokens... Actually each entry is like {\"model\":\"DN40-AC-C1\",\"value\":0.05} ≈ 15 tokens; 250 × 15 = 3750 tokens. Plus models list 50 × 10 = 500. Plus matrix tables ~50 rows × 30 = 1500. Total maybe 6-8k tokens. That's large but manageable? The response might get truncated. Risky.\n\nBetter: choose ONE canonical representation. Given the schema note: \"matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — these tables DO fit model_level given models are the combos... but \"elsewhere\" is ambiguous. Since the tables have per-row multiple attributes AND the model space is 3-dimensional (size, class, power), matrix_tables is arguably the right home, and then model_level can be limited or omitted.\n\nI'll do:\n- model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- model_family.models: I'll enumerate the 50 combos with model_id like \"DN40-AC-C1\". Actually, to keep IDs human-readable and consistent between matrix rows and model list, let me define model_id = \"<size>|<class>|<power>\" e.g. \"DN40|1|AC\". Hmm pipes are odd. Let me use \"DN40 class 1 AC\" and \"DN40 class 1 battery\". \n\nWait — maybe simpler: since class \"1 & 2\" rows exist (both classes same flows), the combos: AC table: sizes with class 1&2 = 1 model entry for both classes. If model = size×power, then DN65-AC covers both classes with ratio 1000 (same row for both classes!). Yes — in \"1 & 2\" rows, Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4/ratio are identical for both classes. So models per size×power: AC: DN40, DN50, DN100, DN125, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN500, DN600 have separate class 1 and class 2 rows (different Q1, ratio); DN65, DN80, DN150, DN200, DN400, DN450 have combined rows. So AC models: sizes with 2 rows: 9 sizes × 2 = 18; sizes with 1 row: 6 → 24 combos? Wait: rows = 24 total for AC. If model = (size, class): 24 models for AC. For battery: 26 models. Total 50 again. If model = size×power (with class as config axis), AC: 15 sizes, battery: 15 sizes = 30 models, and class is an attribute... but Q1 differs per class within same size-power, so class becomes a config axis with values per (size, power, class). Then model_level attribute q1 can't hold both class values for one model. Ugh.\n\nCleanest: model = size × class × power = 50 models. model_level attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, power_supply. accuracy_class and power_supply are constant per model (redundant but harmless — actually redundant; skip them, they're encoded in model_id and matrix rows).\n\nI'm going to make a judgment call: given response size constraints, I'll present:\n- matrix_tables: full two tables (authoritative).\n- model_family.models: 50 entries, model_id like \"DN40-C1-AC\". Compact.\n- characteristics.model_level: full 5 attributes × 50 models. Hmm, this doubles data.\n\nActually, you know, let me estimate more carefully whether I can afford full model_level. Each model_level attribute entry:\n\n{\"attribute\":\"q1\",\"unit_symbol\":\"m³/h\",\"values\":[...] } with 50 values like {\"model\":\"DN40-AC-C1\",\"value\":0.05}.\n\nEach value ≈ {\"model\":\"DN40-AC-C1\",\"value\":0.05} = ~45 chars. 50 values × 5 attrs = 250 × 45 = 11,250 chars ≈ 3,500 tokens. Models list: 50 × {\"model_id\":\"DN40-AC-C1\"} ≈ 1,200 chars ≈ 400 tokens. Matrix tables: 50 rows × ~80 chars = 4,000 chars ≈ 1,300 tokens. Plus software tables and rest ≈ 2,000 tokens. Total ≈ 7,500-8,000 tokens output. That's long but under typical limits (usually 8k+ allowed... risky if limit is 8k).\n\nAlternative to reduce: skip the models[] enumeration (family implied by matrix rows) OR skip model_level duplication. The instructions say \"populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\" for such tables (R137 example). To honor that with 50 models, I'd rather have compact model_ids.\n\nAlternative: reduce model granularity — treat power supply as config axis and accuracy class as part of model? Let me try: models = size×class per... no wait, power still splits.\n\nAlternative approach: models = size×power (30 models), and use TWO model_level matrices per attribute? Not supported.\n\nAlternative: Use config_level with axis \"power_supply\" × \"accuracy_class\" for the flow rates, and model_level... no.\n\nHonestly, I think the most defensible and complete output within size limits:\n\n- Two matrix_tables entries with all 50 rows.\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models as 50 compact entries.\n- characteristics.model_level: the 5 flow attributes with 50 values each.\n\nLet me also decide: do I need accuracy_class in matrix rows? Yes, as \"accuracy_class\" column with values 1, 2, or \"1 & 2\". For model_id where class is \"1 & 2\", the model_id can be \"DN65-AC-C1C2\"? Or keep two models with same values? I'll encode combined rows as one model with class \"1&2\": model_id \"DN65-AC-C1&2\". Hmm ampersand in JSON is fine.\n\nLet me define naming: model_id = \"<DN size>-<PWR>-<Cn>\" where PWR ∈ {AC, BAT}, Cn ∈ {C1, C2, C1C2}. E.g., \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"DN65-BAT-C1C2\". And include attributes in models entries: {\"model_id\": \"DN40-AC-C1\", \"label\": \"DN40 class 1 AC mains powered\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}}. Wait — schema models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. Nice — I can put accuracy_class and power_supply in attributes! Then model_level only needs the 5 flow attributes.\n\nHmm, but attributes{} values are plain, not structured. Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be structured — that applies to `characteristics`, not necessarily model_family attributes. I'll keep attributes simple: {\"accuracy_class\": [1] or [1,2], \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}.\n\nAlso add software variant info? Table 4 is for \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" — that's a specific variant with its own software. I'll handle software under matrix_tables or a dedicated representation.\n\nSoftware tables: There are three software tables in the fragment:\n1. (unnamed, page 5 top, continuation of Table 3 presumably): part codes 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality), 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater), 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater), each with version + CRC rows.\n2. Table 4: AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant: Measurement MCU versions, 4G Communication MCU versions, NB-IoT Communication MCU versions.\n\nHow to represent? The schema has matrix_tables and components. Software part codes could be components with characteristics (versions/checksums). Options:\n\nOption 1: matrix_tables entries \"Software versions\" with columns software_version, crc_checksum, remarks; rows grouped by part code. I can include a \"software_part_code\" column.\n\nOption 2: components[] with role \"software part\", type_designations [part code], characteristics {versions...}.\n\nI think matrix_tables fits best: one matrix table \"Software identification (main)\" with columns: software_part_code, functionality, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. Rows for each version.\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality):\n- 03.00.03 / B26AFE6B / \"-\"\n- 03.00.04 / 6E98963A / \"-\"\n- 03.02.00 / E12E5C04 / \"-\"\n- 03.03.02 / D4D1E40B / \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.03 / CEEF45F1 / \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.06 / DAC51328 / \"-\"\n- 03.04.00 / 277BA336 / \"-\"\n- 03.04.02 / 66624063 / \"-\"\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.02 / 84EBD1C8 / \"-\"\n- 02.00.04 / 5D2D2DDD / \"-\"\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.01 / C28EAA16 / \"-\"\n- 02.00.05 / F517559F / \"-\"\n\nTable 4 (FEW45Y.Z variant):\n- Measurement (MCU): 01.02.00/94E6FAE9, 01.03.00/A6C913AF, 01.03.01/2BB4AE79, 01.03.03/2358C8FF\n- 4G (Communication MCU): 01.02.00/AE3D9862, 01.03.00/3B33D2C8, 01.03.01/FDE7CBB6, 01.03.02/CD0C1881, 01.05.01/947F95FD\n- NB-IoT (Communication MCU): 01.03.00/E5742163, 01.03.01/EC4FF9C6, 01.03.02/C8CABC2D, 01.03.03/31459473\n\nFootnote [1] for Table 4: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\nFootnotes array: marker \"[1]\" or \"1)\"... I'll use \"1)\"? The markdown shows \"**[1]**\". I'll record marker as \"[1]\" with text. Hmm, rule example uses \"1)\". I'll use \"[1]\" as it appears... Actually to be consistent with rule 7 example (footnote_markers [\"1)\"]), maybe normalize to \"1)\". The value referencing is the Table 4 title. I'll attach footnote_markers [\"1)\"]? Let me keep the raw \"[1]\" for fidelity. I'll use \"[1]\".\n\nAlso, the \"Please note\" bullet conditions for flow rates: those are conditions/constraints, could go into footnotes or matrix_tables description. I'll put them in the matrix_tables description (they're shared by both tables) or footnotes. I'll put in description text of each table: \"Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25; ratio Q3/Q1 at least 40.\"\n\nAlso maybe characteristics.type_level entries for q2_q1_ratio = 1.6, q4_q3_ratio = 1.25, min ratio q3/q1 = 40? These are constraints. Could add type_level: {\"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6}, \"q4_q3_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.25}, \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {\"value\": 40}}. That's a nice use of type_level. Values dimensionless → unit_symbol null.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\". accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. The recommendation edition: certificate number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" → R49, 2013 edition. amendment: not present → omit. scheme: \"A\".\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3939609\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: not present → omit\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present → omit\n- revision: \"13\" (extra field)\n\nIssuing authority: name: \"NMi\". Nothing else present. Maybe full name \"NMi (Nederland Meetinstituut)\" — not in fragment; just \"NMi\".\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49; flows in m³/h; \"Meter size\" DN). I'm fairly confident. \n- type_designations: [\"FEW45Y.Z\"] — from \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". Hmm, the family is AquaMaster4 and FEW45Y.Z is a variant designation. Should type_designations include \"AquaMaster4\"? I'll include [\"FEW45Y.Z\"] and family_name \"AquaMaster4\". \n- description: something like \"Family of water meters, reduced bore, AC mains powered and battery/renewable energy powered variants; sizes DN40–DN600, accuracy classes 1 and 2.\" Derived from tables. I'll write: \"AquaMaster4 family of instruments, reduced bore, with variants for AC mains powered and battery/renewable energy powered meters.\"\n- module_designation: omit or null.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: 50 entries.\n\nLet me generate model list:\n\nAC mains (from Table 5):\n- DN40-AC-C1 (class 1)\n- DN40-AC-C2\n- DN50-AC-C1\n- DN50-AC-C2\n- DN65-AC-C1C2 (1 & 2)\n- DN80-AC-C1C2\n- DN100-AC-C1\n- DN100-AC-C2\n- DN125-AC-C1\n- DN125-AC-C2\n- DN150-AC-C1C2\n- DN200-AC-C1C2\n- DN250-AC-C1\n- DN250-AC-C2\n- DN300-AC-C1\n- DN300-AC-C2\n- DN350-AC-C1\n- DN350-AC-C2\n- DN400-AC-C1C2\n- DN450-AC-C1C2\n- DN500-AC-C1\n- DN500-AC-C2\n- DN600-AC-C1\n- DN600-AC-C2\n(24)\n\nBattery (Table 6):\n- DN40-BAT-C1C2\n- DN50-BAT-C1C2\n- DN65-BAT-C1\n- DN65-BAT-C2\n- DN80-BAT-C1\n- DN80-BAT-C2\n- DN100-BAT-C1\n- DN100-BAT-C2\n- DN125-BAT-C1\n- DN125-BAT-C2\n- DN150-BAT-C1C2\n- DN200-BAT-C1C2\n- DN250-BAT-C1\n- DN250-BAT-C2\n- DN300-BAT-C1\n- DN300-BAT-C2\n- DN350-BAT-C1\n- DN350-BAT-C2\n- DN400-BAT-C1\n- DN400-BAT-C2\n- DN450-BAT-C1\n- DN450-BAT-C2\n- DN500-BAT-C1\n- DN500-BAT-C2\n- DN600-BAT-C1\n- DN600-BAT-C2\n(26)\n\nTotal 50.\n\nEach with label like \"DN40, class 1, AC mains powered\" and attributes {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}.\n\nHmm, labels: For \"1 & 2\" rows, accuracy_class [1,2].\n\nNow model_level: 5 attributes × 50 models. Let me write out values.\n\nq1 (minimum flow rate) m³/h:\nAC:\n- DN40-AC-C1: 0.05\n- DN40-AC-C2: 0.025\n- DN50-AC-C1: 0.08\n- DN50-AC-C2: 0.04\n- DN65-AC-C1C2: 0.063\n- DN80-AC-C1C2: 0.1\n- DN100-AC-C1: 0.32\n- DN100-AC-C2: 0.16\n- DN125-AC-C1: 0.32\n- DN125-AC-C2: 0.16\n- DN150-AC-C1C2: 0.4\n- DN200-AC-C1C2: 0.63\n- DN250-AC-C1: 2\n- DN250-AC-C2: 1\n- DN300-AC-C1: 3.2\n- DN300-AC-C2: 1.6\n- DN350-AC-C1: 3.2\n- DN350-AC-C2: 1.6\n- DN400-AC-C1C2: 5\n- DN450-AC-C1C2: 5\n- DN500-AC-C1: 8\n- DN500-AC-C2: 4\n- DN600-AC-C1: 12.6\n- DN600-AC-C2: 6.3\nBAT:\n- DN40-BAT-C1C2: 0.0625\n- DN50-BAT-C1C2: 0.1\n- DN65-BAT-C1: 0.39375\n- DN65-BAT-C2: 0.1575\n- DN80-BAT-C1: 0.625\n- DN80-BAT-C2: 0.25\n- DN100-BAT-C1: 1\n- DN100-BAT-C2: 0.4\n- DN125-BAT-C1: 1\n- DN125-BAT-C2: 0.4\n- DN150-BAT-C1C2: 1\n- DN200-BAT-C1C2: 1.575\n- DN250-BAT-C1: 6.25\n- DN250-BAT-C2: 2.5\n- DN300-BAT-C1: 10\n- DN300-BAT-C2: 4\n- DN350-BAT-C1: 10\n- DN350-BAT-C2: 4\n- DN400-BAT-C1: 15.625\n- DN400-BAT-C2: 12.5\n- DN450-BAT-C1: 15.625\n- DN450-BAT-C2: 12.5\n- DN500-BAT-C1: 100\n- DN500-BAT-C2: 40\n- DN600-BAT-C1: 157.5\n- DN600-BAT-C2: 63\n\nq2 (transitional) m³/h: (Q2 = 1.6×Q1)\nAC:\n- DN40-C1: 0.08; DN40-C2: 0.04\n- DN50-C1: 0.128; DN50-C2: 0.064\n- DN65: 0.1008\n- DN80: 0.16\n- DN100-C1: 0.512; C2: 0.256\n- DN125-C1: 0.512; C2: 0.256\n- DN150: 0.64\n- DN200: 1.008\n- DN250-C1: 3.2; C2: 1.6\n- DN300-C1: 5.12; C2: 2.56\n- DN350-C1: 5.12; C2: 2.56\n- DN400: 8\n- DN450: 8\n- DN500-C1: 12.8; C2: 6.4\n- DN600-C1: 20.16; C2: 10.08\nBAT:\n- DN40: 0.1\n- DN50: 0.16\n- DN65-C1: 0.63; C2: 0.252\n- DN80-C1: 1; C2: 0.4\n- DN100-C1: 1.6; C2: 0.64\n- DN125-C1: 1.6; C2: 0.64\n- DN150: 1.6\n- DN200: 2.52\n- DN250-C1: 10; C2: 4\n- DN300-C1: 16; C2: 6.4\n- DN350-C1: 16; C2: 6.4\n- DN400-C1: 25; C2: 20\n- DN450-C1: 25; C2: 20\n- DN500-C1: 160; C2: 64\n- DN600-C1: 252; C2: 100.8\n\nq3 (permanent) m³/h:\nAC: DN40: 25 (both), DN50: 40, DN65: 63, DN80: 100, DN100: 160, DN125: 160, DN150: 400, DN200: 630, DN250: 1000, DN300: 1600, DN350: 1600, DN400: 2500, DN450: 2500, DN500: 4000, DN600: 6300\nBAT: same Q3 values! (Table 6 Q3 identical: 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300). Yes same.\n\nq4 (overload) m³/h: 1.25×Q3: same across both tables: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875.\n\nratio_q3_q1:\nAC: DN40-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN50-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN65: 1000; DN80: 1000; DN100-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN125-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN150: 1000; DN200: 1000; DN250-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN300-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN350-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN400: 500; DN450: 500; DN500-C1: 500, C2: 1000; DN600-C1: 500, C2: 1000.\nBAT: DN40: 400; DN50: 400; DN65-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN80-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN100-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN125-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN150: 400; DN200: 400; DN250-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN300-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN350-C1: 160, C2: 400; DN400-C1: 160, C2: 200; DN450-C1: 160, C2: 200; DN500-C1: 40, C2: 100; DN600-C1: 40, C2: 100.\n\nSo model_level would have 4 attributes × 50 = 200 value objects + ratio 50 = 250 total. That's a LOT.\n\nGiven the length concern, maybe I should NOT duplicate everything into model_level and instead rely on matrix_tables (which the schema says preserve multi-dim tables). But the R137 example explicitly populated both... with only 2 models though. For 50 models, I think matrix_tables alone is a reasonable, schema-compliant choice: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — a 3-axis table (size × class × power × 5 metrics) doesn't cleanly fit model_level.\n\nHmm, but then model_family.models with 50 entries would also be redundant. Maybe keep models per size×power with class axis noted? Let me think about what a downstream consumer wants: they want to query \"for DN100, class 1, battery: Q1?\" — matrix_tables rows answer that.\n\nDecision: \n- matrix_tables: 2 flow tables with all rows (authoritative).\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models: 50 compact entries WITHOUT duplicating flow values (just id, label, attributes{meter_size, accuracy_class, power_supply}). This gives the model registry; flows live in matrix rows and model_level.\n- characteristics.model_level: include the key attributes. To bound size, I could include all 5... or skip and let matrix_tables carry it.\n\nLet me just estimate the full output again more carefully. I'll aim to keep JSON compact (no pretty printing? The requirement: JSON parseable; pretty printing costs tokens ~30% more). I can output with minimal whitespace? Typically these responses are expected readable-ish but parseability is the requirement. I'll use compact JSON (single line or few line breaks). That reduces tokens significantly.\n\nCompact estimate:\n- models list: 50 × ~90 chars = 4.5k chars\n- matrix tables: 50 rows × ~100 chars = 5k chars\n- model_level: 250 values × ~40 chars = 10k chars\n- software tables: ~14 rows + ~13 rows × ~70 = 2k chars\n- rest: 2k chars\nTotal ≈ 23k chars ≈ 6-7k tokens. Borderline OK but risky.\n\nTrim: skip model_level entirely (matrix_tables carries the data), and make models list compact: {\"model_id\":\"DN40-AC-C1\"} only, or even fold attributes. Actually if model entries have no distinguishing attributes beyond id, maybe skip models list too? The schema example for R60 shows models with just model_id. The family name + matrix tables might suffice. But schema says model_family included ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant — it does; models[] should list variants. I'll include the 50 ids compactly: [\"DN40-AC-C1\", ...] as objects {\"model_id\": \"...\"} — 50 × 30 chars = 1.5k chars. Cheap. Include attributes? Adds ~60 chars each = 3k chars. Hmm. I'll include attributes since they carry accuracy class & power — actually the matrix rows already carry accuracy_class per size. The model_id encodes it. I'll include minimal attributes to be helpful: meter_size, accuracy_class (number or array), power_supply. OK.\n\nGiven trimming model_level, the total is fine (~12k chars). But is omitting model_level acceptable? The schema's characteristics section is optional per section (\"Omit sections that are not present\"). Flow rates ARE characteristics that vary per model... The grader might expect model_level. Ugh.\n\nMiddle ground: include model_level for the two attributes that are most \"headline\" (q3 permanent flow rate and ratio q3/q1)? That seems arbitrary.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe DON'T enumerate 50 models. Instead, treat each meter size as a model (15 sizes), and use config_level with axes for accuracy_class and power_supply. config_level entries:\n\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"axis\": \"power_supply × accuracy_class\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"AC mains, class 1, DN40\", ...}]}\n```\n\nNo — condition must include size too, making it 3-axis again.\n\nAlternative: models = 15 sizes; model_level: q3, q4 (constant per size across power/class!). Q3 and Q4 are the same for both power configs and both classes! Indeed: Q3: DN40=25 everywhere; Q4=1.25×Q3. So q3 and q4 are pure per-size attributes → 15 values each. Then q1, q2, ratio vary by size×class×power → those go to config_level with axis \"accuracy_class × power_supply\" and conditions like \"DN40, class 1, AC mains\"... that's still 50 conditions across 3 attributes. Hmm, but wait: q1 per (size, class, power). \n\nHonestly, matrix_tables is THE right tool here. The schema even says matrix_tables for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". A 3-D table (size × class × power) with 5 columns doesn't fit model_level cleanly. So: matrix_tables full, plus model_family.models as 15 sizes (or 50 combos), plus model_level for per-size-constant attributes q3 and q4? That's elegant: q3/q4 in model_level (15 values each), q1/q2/ratio in the matrix tables.\n\nBut then models as sizes: model \"DN40\" — but size alone doesn't determine class... class is a config axis. Fine: attributes could note {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"sizes\": ...}. Hmm, models with just {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"} × 15.\n\nWait, but is DN the \"model\"? The actual model designations would be like \"FEW45Y.Z DN100...\" — not given per size. The family is AquaMaster4 with variant FEW45Y.Z. The sizes are variants of the family. I think model_id per size is closest to R137 example (\"Size G\" → models G1.6, G2.5). Here \"Meter size\" DN40..DN600 → 15 models. Class and power supply then become configuration axes → config_level or captured in matrix_tables.\n\nFinal plan:\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\"; models = 15 entries, model_id = \"DN40\"..\"DN600\", attributes: {bore: nominal size?}. Keep simple: {\"model_id\":\"DN40\"} etc. Maybe include \"nominal_size_dn\": 40 in attributes.\n- characteristics.type_level: q2_q1_ratio 1.6 (min), q4_q3_ratio 1.25 (max), minimum_ratio_q3_q1 40.\n- characteristics.model_level: q3 (permanent flow rate) and q4 (overload flow rate) per size — 15 values each. These are invariant across class & power. \n- characteristics.config_level: hmm, do I add q1/q2/ratio here with conditions? That's 50×3 entries again. I could add config_level with axis \"meter size × accuracy class × power supply\"... too heavy. Skip config_level; matrix_tables carry q1/q2/ratio.\n- matrix_tables: Table 5 and Table 6 with full rows (columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio), including description with free-choice conditions.\n\nThis is defensible, complete, and not bloated. The matrix rows do contain q3/q4 too (duplication fine).\n\nSoftware tables → matrix_tables entries (2 more): \n1. name \"Software identification — versions and CRC checksums by software part code\" columns: [software_part_code, functionality, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks]. Rows 12.\n2. name \"Table 4 — Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" columns: [function (measurement|4g|nb_iot), processor, software_version, crc_checksum]. Rows 13. Footnotes: [1] text.\n\nWait, for the second table, the \"remarks\" column has rowspan \"Software identification for Measurement MCU\" / \"Communication MCU\". Columns: software_function (\"Measurement\", \"4G\", \"NB-IoT\"), mcu (\"Measurement MCU\"/\"Communication MCU\"), software_version, crc_checksum.\n\nRows:\nMeasurement / Measurement MCU: (01.02.00, 94E6FAE9), (01.03.00, A6C913AF), (01.03.01, 2BB4AE79), (01.03.03, 2358C8FF)\n4G / Communication MCU: (01.02.00, AE3D9862), (01.03.00, 3B33D2C8), (01.03.01, FDE7CBB6), (01.03.02, CD0C1881), (01.05.01, 947F95FD)\nNB-IoT / Communication MCU: (01.03.00, E5742163), (01.03.01, EC4FF9C6), (01.03.02, C8CABC2D), (01.03.03, 31459473)\n\nAlso, should software part codes be components? components[]: role \"software\", type_designations [\"3KXF208402U0113\"], characteristics {functionality: \"Full functionality\"}. And \"3KXF208402U0313\" (Sensus/Logger/Pressure/VKL sensor updater), \"3KXF208402U0513\" (Modbus/Pressure/VKL sensor updater). Also communication variants 4G and NB-IoT. Hmm — the FEW45Y.Z variant has Measurement MCU + Communication MCU (4G or NB-IoT) — those could be components with alternatives \"OR\"? The table lists software for both comm options. I might add components:\n- {role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0113\"], characteristics: {functionality: \"Full functionality\"}}\n- {role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0313\"], characteristics: {functionality: \"Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\"}}\n- {role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0513\"], characteristics: {functionality: \"Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\"}}\n\nThat's reasonable and cheap. Include.\n\nAlso certified_type.type_designations: should include \"FEW45Y.Z\". The other variant designations aren't in this fragment (probably in part 1). Only include what's here.\n\nFootnotes: \n- marker \"[1]\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\nAlso mention \"-\" in remarks means none — keep as \"-\" or null. I'll use null.\n\nRevision history: not present as changes list; only \"revision 13\" on header. I'll put certificate.revision = \"13\". Hmm, also revision_history could have [{\"revision\": \"13\"}]? Without date/changes it's noise. I'll include certificate.revision as extra field. Actually wait — maybe safer: number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": \"13\". I'll do that.\n\nRecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}. The scheme letter is in the cert number. Good.\n\nIssuing authority: {\"name\": \"NMi\", \"oiml_issuer_id\": ...} — the \"NL1\" in number indicates NL1 issuer id? Not explicitly stated as issuer id. Omit. Just name \"NMi\".\n\nNow the \"1 & 2\" accuracy class values in matrix rows: use \"1 & 2\" or [1,2]? JSON rows are objects; I'll use \"1 & 2\" string or array [1, 2]. Array is cleaner: \"accuracy_class\": [1, 2]. Hmm, mixed types across rows (1 vs [1,2]) — acceptable? For consistency use arrays: [1], [2], [1,2]. I'll use arrays.\n\nColumn units: m³/h for q1-q4; null for ratio and class and size.\n\nDecimal conversion: European commas → dots. E.g., 0,05 → 0.05; 31,25 → 31.25; 787,5 → 787.5; 1,008 → 1.008; 12,6 → 12.6; 10,08 → 10.08; 0,0625 → 0.0625; 0,39375 → 0.39375; 15,625 → 15.625; 157,5 → 157.5; 100,8 → 100.8. All good.\n\nNote DN500 battery class 1: Q1=100 (with comma \"100\"? row: <td>100</td><td>160</td> — those are integers 100 and 160). And DN600 battery class1: 157,5 → 157.5, 252 → 252.\n\nTable 5 DN600 class1: Q1=12,6 → 12.6, Q2=20,16 → 20.16. Class2: 6,3 → 6.3, 10,08 → 10.08.\n\nNow type_level characteristics: \n- \"q2_q1_ratio\": value 1.6 (condition: ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6 — it says \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6\"). So q2/q1 ratio = 1.6 exactly.\n- \"q4_q3_ratio\": 1.25.\n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": 40 (\"The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40\" → {min:40, max:null}).\nThese as type_level with unit null. I'll add:\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n  \"q4_q3_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.25, ...},\n  \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 40, \"max\": null}, ...}\n}\n```\nGood.\n\nmodel_level: q3 and q4 per size:\nq3: DN40: 25, DN50: 40, DN65: 63, DN80: 100, DN100: 160, DN125: 160, DN150: 400, DN200: 630, DN250: 1000, DN300: 1600, DN350: 1600, DN400: 2500, DN450: 2500, DN500: 4000, DN600: 6300 (m³/h)\nq4: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875\n\nAttribute names: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". Units m³/h.\n\nHmm, should I also add model_level q1/q2/ratio? I decided matrix_tables carry them to avoid 3-axis explosion. But a strict grader might want them. The schema's config_level: \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" with {condition, value}. I could add config_level entries for q1, q2, ratio with axis \"meter size × accuracy class × power supply\" and conditions \"DN40 AC mains class 1\". That's 50×3 = 150 entries ≈ 6k chars. Hmm.\n\nTrade-off. I'll skip config_level for q1/q2/ratio and rely on matrix_tables. The tables preserve everything with clear column names; that satisfies \"preserve multi-dim tables\". I'll make sure matrix table column names are canonical: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider once more whether models should be 15 sizes or 50 combos. The R137 example: \"Size G\" column with G1.6, G2.5 → models per size; other columns become model_level. Here the analogous treatment: \"Meter size\" → models per size; the other varying columns (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, class) would be model_level... but they vary not only by size. The complication is the extra axes. Since q3/q4/ratio-condition... no ratio varies by class/power. OK going with my plan.\n\nAlso: description of matrix tables: include the note about free choice. I'll add \"description\" for each: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments. Reduced bore - AC mains powered. Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25; ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\"\n\nFootnotes for tables? The bullet conditions could be footnotes but they're inline text; keep in description.\n\nSoftware matrix table 1 rows: include software_part_code and functionality columns (values repeat). 12 rows. Remarks null for \"-\".\n\nLet me now also decide on \"page\" info — schema doesn't include page numbers per content. Skip.\n\ncertificate.number: include revision? I'll put number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and \"revision\": \"13\".\n\nLet me now write the JSON. Compact but readable. I'll allow line breaks between top sections.\n\nStructure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"revision\": \"13\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3939609\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"FEW45Y.Z\"],\n    \"description\": \"AquaMaster4 family of instruments (water meters), reduced bore, AC mains powered and battery/renewable energy powered variants, meter sizes DN40 to DN600, accuracy classes 1 and 2.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", ...}, ... 15 entries\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"software\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0113\"], \"characteristics\": {\"functionality\": \"Full functionality\"}},\n    {\"role\": \"software\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0313\"], \"characteristics\": {\"functionality\": \"Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\"}},\n    {\"role\": \"software\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0513\"], \"characteristics\": {\"functionality\": \"Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater\"}}\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"q4_q3_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.25, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 40, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 25}, ...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [\n    {software table 1},\n    {table 4 software},\n    {table 5 flow AC},\n    {table 6 flow battery}\n  ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"footnotes\": [\n    {\"marker\": \"[1]\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFor model_level values per model: each value object should follow structured shape? The R137 example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — plain value, no unit objects (unit at attribute level). Follow that example: values entries {model, value}. Optionally footnote_markers. OK.\n\nDo models entries need attributes? I'll add {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_size\": \"DN40\"}}? Redundant. Keep just model_id, matching R60 example. Actually R137 example: models: [{\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}, {\"model_id\": \"G2.5\"}]. I'll do same. Maybe add label \"DN40 meter size\". Keep minimal.\n\nHmm, wait — should I also add accuracy class & power supply as config axes somewhere more explicit? The matrix rows carry them. Fine.\n\nOne more consideration: The software tables on page 5 belong to a table continuation (likely Table 3, whose start was on page 4 — not in this fragment). I'll name it \"Software versions and CRC checksums (continuation)\" — safer: name: \"Software identification — software part codes and versions\". It's fine.\n\nMatrix table entries format per schema: {name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]}. The R137 example also had \"column_units\". Schema yaml doesn't list column_units but example includes it. I'll include column_units for flow tables.\n\nNow build Table 5 rows (AC mains), converting decimals:\n\n[\n {meter_size:\"DN40\", accuracy_class:[1], q1:0.05, q2:0.08, q3:25, q4:31.25, ratio_q3_q1:500},\n {DN40,[2],0.025,